---
description: >
  Alignment drift after the cold-start install refactor: stale scope path,
  prior aligned stamp, and intent paragraph referring to "the init subtree"
  rather than the install service subtree.
intent: >
  initContent's spec was previously stamped aligned under the
  src/tools/init/ regime. After the move to src/service/install/ the path
  is stale, and the intent's "init subtree" terminology hardcodes the old
  module identity.
misalignment:
  - spec-gap
---

## Issues

- [ ] `src/service/install/initContent/.aide` frontmatter
  `scope: src/tools/init/initContent`
  Stale path — module moved to `src/service/install/initContent/`.
  Traces to: physical move; ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` ->
  outcomes.desired[4] (folder tree as architecture).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/initContent/.aide` intent paragraph and
  outcomes: repeated phrase "the init subtree", "init helpers", "init/"
  Identity drift — the module is no longer named `init`. The terminology
  "init subtree" was correct under `src/tools/init/`; under
  `src/service/install/` it should be "the install service subtree" or
  "install helpers". This is the chokepoint single-reader invariant; the
  invariant applies to whichever subtree contains the helpers, but the
  spec hardcodes the old subtree name.
  Traces to: physical move; ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` ->
  outcomes.desired[2] ("composed from the canonical doc files at build or
  init time, never duplicated") — the chokepoint claim is correct, just
  scoped to a name that no longer exists.
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/initContent/.aide` Bad examples mention
  "writeMethodology bypasses initContent and opens `docs/aide-spec.md`
  directly"
  Locations of sibling helpers (writeMethodology, scaffoldCommands,
  installMethodologyDocs) are all under `src/service/install/` now. The
  examples remain semantically valid but reference paths that, when
  resolved, point at moved files.
  Traces to: physical move; ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` ->
  outcomes.undesired[3] (aspirational docs).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

## Retro

Same root cause as the parent. The stale `aligned` stamp this spec
carried was correct under the old folder layout — it is the architectural
shift, not a content drift, that invalidated it. Any folder-rename of a
spec's directory should auto-clear `aligned` to `pending` so the next
alignment pass re-stamps it deliberately.
